\subsection{Integrable system consistent with re-parametrisations}

Let us introduce the functions:
\bea
f_1&=\dfrac{s_3}{s_1 s_2};\quad f_2&=\dfrac{s_5}{s_2 s_4};\nn\\
f_3&=s^2_1 s_4, \quad f_4&=s_2 s_4^2.\nn
\eea
These functions transform in a rather special way under re-parametrisation on the unipotent group. By evident arguments  we get:
\begin{lemma}
\label{lem_act}
The functions $f_3$ and $f_4$ are invariants of the action of $T_{ijk}$.
In addition:
\bea
T_{123}(f_1)=f_1+1;\qquad T_{356} (f_2)=f_2+1.\nn
\eea
All other transformations preserve $f_1, f_2.$
\end{lemma}

\begin{thm}
Consider the involutive subalgebra $(f_1,\ldots, f_4)$ in the case of the Poisson structure $I_1$ and the level set of the Casimir elements
\bea
\mathcal{M}_c=\{M\in U(4,\R), f_1(M)=c_1, f_2(M)=c_2\}.\nn
\eea
The functions $f_3,f_4$ define an integrable system on $\mathcal{M}_c$, moreover, this system is compatible with the parametrisation change transformations $T_{ijk}.$
\end{thm}

\begin{rem}
The proof of this fact is a direct consequence of the fact that the parametrisation change transformations of the unipotent group that we study preserve the Poisson structure and the Hamiltonians. More interesting is the observation that the transformations $T_{ijk}$ act non-trivially on the level submanifolds of the Hamiltonians by Lemma \ref{lem_act}.
\end{rem}
