\section{Introduction} 
Hilbert schemes of points are natural and crucial spaces in algebraic geometry. However, they are known to be highly singular. There has been tremendous growth in research to understand the singularities and behaviour of these spaces.  For example, for Murphy's law for the Hilbert scheme, see \cite{Vakil06}, and for a proof for the Hilbert schemes of points,  see \cite{Jelisiejew20}; for the Hilbert scheme of points on threefolds, see \cite{Ramkumar-Sammartano,Jelisiejew-Ramkumar-Sammartano24, HuX, KatzS}; for the reducibility, see \cite{Iarrobino72, Hilb8, Jardim, Hilb_11}; for rational singularities, see \cite{Ramkumar-Sammartano24}; for irrational components of the Hilbert scheme of points, see \cite{Farkas-Pandharipande-Sammartano24}; for the parity conjecture, see \cite{PandharipandeSlides,Ramkumar-Sammartano1,GGGL23}; for other works related to enumerative geometry, see \cite{BBS, BFHilb,JKSCounterBehrend, MNOP1, Nesterov25, PT, RicolfiSign}.  



Because of the extremely singular nature of the Hilbert schemes, measuring the singularity is of great importance.  Let $N\geq 3$. The dimension of the tangent space to $\mathrm{Hilb}^l(\BA^N)$ at each point measures how singular the point is. If $l$ is a tetrahedral number ${N-1+k\choose N}$,  Brian\c{c}on and Iarrobino predicted the most singular points of the Hilbert scheme:

\begin{conj}\cite[Brian\c{c}on-Iarrobino, 1978]{Bri-Iar} \label{1978Conj} The most singular point of $\Hilb^{{N-1+k \choose N}}(\BA^N)$ corresponds to the ideal
 $\mathfrak{m}^k$, where $\mathfrak{m}$ is the maximal ideal in $\BC[x_1,x_2,\dots,x_N]$.
\end{conj}


This conjecture is still open. To pave the way towards proving this long-standing conjecture, investigating the relationship between the shape of the ideals corresponding to the points of the Hilbert scheme and their level of singularity was suggested in \cite{Rezaee-23-Conjectures}. In this article, we further generalize the necessary condition in \cite{Rezaee-23-Conjectures} using a novel and stunning pattern in the shape of the \textit{locally-maximal singularities} (see Definition \ref{def: local-max}).

Let $A=\BC[x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_N]$. The statement of \cite[Lemma 1.7]{Rezaee-23-Conjectures} implies that if we prove the following necessary condition for being the most singular point of the Hilbert scheme (\cite[Conjecture B]{Rezaee-23-Conjectures}), then, in particular, we can resolve Conjecture \ref{1978Conj}.


\begin{conj}\cite[Conjecture B]{Rezaee-23-Conjectures}  \label{necessaryCondition}For $N\geq 3$,   let $I$ be a 0-dimensional Borel-fixed ideal of colength $l$ in $A$  given by
   \begin{align*}
       I=(x_1^{m_1},x_2^{m_2}, \ldots,x_N^{m_N},\text{mixed monomial generators}),
   \end{align*}
   where  $m_1\leq m_2\leq\ldots\leq m_N$. 

   If ${N-1+k \choose N}\leq l<{N+k\choose N}$, and $I$ correspond to the most singular point of $\mathrm{Hilb}^{l}(\BA^N)$, then $m_1=k$.

\end{conj}

We conjecturally extend the  Brian\c{c}on-Iarrobino conjecture on the maximal dimension of the tangent space to the Hilbert scheme of points in 3D. 
We propose a weaker version of this conjecture, which will still be enough to conclude Conjecture \ref{1978Conj}.

\subsection*{Notation}
   Apart from the notations above, for fixed (co)length $l$ and the smallest pure exponent $m_1$ (which, by the above convention, is the exponent of the first variable $x_1$), we denote by $T_{\max,m_1}(l)$, the maximal dimension of the tangent space to $\mathrm{Hilb}^l(\BA^3)$, among points corresponding to the Bore-fixed ideals ideals of (co)length $l$ and the exponent of the first variable $m_1$. Also, we have
\begin{align*}
    T_{\max}(l)=\max\{  T_{\max,1}(l),  T_{\max,2}(l),\ldots,  T_{\max,k}(l)\}.
\end{align*}
  \subsection*{Terminology} When we say \textit{the dimension of the tangent space of an ideal $I$}, we mean the dimension of the tangent space of the Hilbert scheme $\Hilb^{\colength(I)}(\BA^2)$ at the zero locus of $I$.


