\paragraph{Formal Results and Background.} We consider a linear setting where, for $X \in \R^d$, $\mathbb{E}_P[Y|X] = {\theta^*_P}^\top X$ while $\mathbb{E}_Q[Y|X] = {\theta^*_Q}^\top X$, and where $\theta^*_P$ and $\theta^*_Q$ may be different. The learner is given two labeled samples $S_P, S_Q$ from $P, Q$, of respective sizes $n_P, n_Q$ (a typical regime of interest is one where $n_Q \ll n_P$, though not essential to our results). Several recent results \cite{mousavi2020minimax, zhang2022class, ge2023maximum, pathak2023noisy, chen2024high} have shown that the statistical hardness of transferring information from $P$ to $Q$ can be characterized by two essential quantities, namely {\rm (i)} $\lambda_{\max}(\Sigma^{-1}_P \Sigma_Q)$, for $\Sigma_P \doteq \mathbb{E}_P XX^\top, \Sigma_Q \doteq \mathbb{E}_Q XX^\top$, and {\rm (ii)} ${\mathcal E}_Q (\theta^*_P) \doteq \|\theta^*_P - \theta^*_Q\|_{\Sigma_Q}^2$. The first quantity remains relevant even when $\theta^*_P = \theta^*_Q$ and upper bounds error ratios $\|\theta-\theta^*_P\|_{\Sigma_Q}^2/\|\theta-\theta^*_P\|_{\Sigma_P}^2$ w.r.t. $\theta^*_P$. 

$\bullet$ We show that, w.h.p., the mixed-samples SGD procedure yields a solution $\hat \theta_{PQ}$, close to the solution $\tilde \theta_{PQ}$ of the constrained optimization \eqref{eq:informalConstrainedObjective}, and hence satisfies  
$$ {\mathcal E}_Q(\hat \theta_{PQ}) \lesssim \min\{ \epsilon_Q, \lambda_{\max}(\Sigma^{-1}_P \Sigma_Q)\cdot \epsilon_P + {\mathcal E}_Q (\theta^*_P) \},$$
for $\epsilon_Q \approx {d}/{n_Q}, \epsilon_P \approx {d}/{n_P}$, 
without prior knowledge of problem parameters. Importantly, the above bound guarantees automatic adaptation to whichever of the source or target data is most beneficial to the target task. In a recently arXived result of \cite{hanneke2024more} a similarly adaptive bound was shown to hold for the solution of a constraint satisfaction problem---having to do with estimating the intersection of certain convex confidence sets---but no concrete procedure is provided.  

$\bullet$ As so far alluded to, the mixed-sample SGD analyzed here aims to efficiently approximate the solution of the \emph{quadratically constrained quadratic program} (QCQP) of equation \eqref{eq:informalConstrainedObjective}, while {(\rm i)} avoiding costly projections onto the constraint set at each iteration step---as would be the case, e.g., with \emph{projected GD or SGD}, and {(\rm ii)} while computing in parallel the solution to 
$\min_{\theta'} \hat R_Q(\theta)$ defining the constraint. 

To this end, we build on insights from a classic projection-efficient SGD approach of \cite{NIPS2012_c52f1bd6}, which aims to solve QCQP of the form $\min F(\theta) \text{ s.t. } G(\theta) \leq 0$ by simultaneously solving for the solution $(\lambda^*, \theta^*)$ of the Lagrangian problem $\max_\lambda \min_{\theta} F(\theta) + \lambda G(\theta)$. However, their approach assumes a bounded constraint set $\{G \leq 0\}$, along with full access to $\nabla G$ to ensure it can approximately maintain the constraint while iterating on $(\lambda_t, \theta_t)$. 

Here, the constraint set in \eqref{eq:informalConstrainedObjective} would be unbounded whenever $\Sigma_Q$ is not full rank as is common in practice. We therefore cannot rely on the approach of \cite{NIPS2012_c52f1bd6}---which efficiently projects each $w_t$ to an $\ell_2$ ball containing the constraint set. Instead, we show that one can carefully track how far iterates $\theta_t$ may deviate from the constraint set in terms of the first iterate distance and SGD variance. However, such control is further complicated by the fact that $\min_{\theta'} \hat R_Q(\theta)$ is not known at first: our mixed-sample SGD procedure instead only knows the constraint set over-time as it solves for $\min_{\theta'} \hat R_Q(\theta)$ in parallel. A main novelty is thus in showing that such a procedure still achieves a similar $1/\sqrt{T}$ convergence rate as classical projection-efficient methods despite the less structured setting with imperfect access to the constraint set. 

