% Here we make few remarks of our main result. First, Theorem~\ref{thm:main result} shows that our Algorithm~\ref{algorithm: SGD with Alternating Sampling} returns a generalizable solution after certain iterations of stochastic gradient updates.  The projection is only needed at the last step so it is projection efficient, compared to classical method such as projected SGD which requires $T$ times of projection. Our convergence rate matches with that of classic projection-efficient optimization framework~\citep{NIPS2012_c52f1bd6}. However, there are three key differences between our approach and theirs. First, they assume the constraint set is bounded and can be covered by an $\ell_2$ ball of radius $B^2$, and in their algorithm they require normalize the $\ell_2$ norm of the iterates every iteration. In contrast, in our setting, the constraint can be unbounded, e.g., when $\Sigma_Q$ is non-invertible. We address this unboundedness via a more careful learning rate design, ensuring that the iterates will never leave the $\ell_2$ ball of radius $2\rho^2$ centered at $\tilde\theta_{PQ}$. Second, in their work, they assume access to full gradient of the constraint function and gradient evaluation of both objective and constraint functions, while our algorithm only require stochastic gradient of constraint function, and alternates between evaluating the stochastic gradient of objective and constraint functions at each iteration. Finally, their approach assumes access to the exact constraint function, whereas our algorithm works with the inexact constraint of Problem~\ref{eq:obj} and iteratively refines the constraint during the optimization process.






